ights into implementing multi-agent systems, prompt engineering for code generation, and best practices for integrating AI agents into development pipelines.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"CodePick.dev\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Technical Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://codepick.dev/en/guides/ai-coding-agents-2026-roadmap/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-11\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"AI agents\", \"workflow design\", \"prompt engineering\", \"multi-agent systems\", \"development pipelines\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"Why Write Code in 2026\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion about the role of human programmers in 2026. Covers LLMs undergoing reinforcement learning on coding, challenges with assembly generation and token inefficiency, and how AI is changing systems thinking and auditing practices.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Hacker News Community\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Hacker News\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48861923\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding\", \"LLM training\", \"reinforcement learning\", \"systems thinking\", \"future of programming\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Best AI Coding Tools July 2026: Post-GPT-5.6 Rankings\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Comprehensive ranking of AI coding tools following the release of GPT-5.6. Claude Code emerges as the top choice, with Cursor holding the #2 IDE spot after the Cursor 3 rebuild. OpenCode is listed as the most practical free option. The analysis reflects the rapidly evolving landscape of AI-assisted development tools.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Agensi.io\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Technical Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://www.agensi.io/learn/best-ai-coding-tools-july-2026\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding tools\", \"Claude Code\", \"Cursor\", \"GPT-5.6\", \"developer tools\", \"IDE\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"The new GPT-5.6 family: Luna, Terra, Sol\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"OpenAI's latest flagship model hit general availability on July 9, 2026, coming in three sizes: Luna, Terra, and Sol (from smallest to largest). Simon Willison provides analysis of the new model family and its implications for AI development.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"GPT-5.6\", \"OpenAI\", \"LLM release\", \"model sizes\", \"AI development\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"State of AI in 2026: LLMs, Coding, Scaling Laws, China, Agents, GPUs, AGI\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"Comprehensive 4.5-hour discussion with Nathan Lambert and Sebastian Raschka covering the current state of AI technology. Topics include closed vs open LLMs, programming & dev tooling, scaling laws, China's role in AI, AI agents, GPU developments, and paths to AGI. The episode provides deep insights into how AI is reshaping software development and the broader technology landscape.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Lex Fridman with Nathan Lambert and Sebastian Raschka\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Podcast\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EV7WhVT270Q\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"LLMs\", \"AI coding\", \"scaling laws\", \"AI agents\", \"GPUs\", \"AGI\", \"developer tools\", \"open source\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"Best AI Coding Agents (July 2026): Scored Leaderboard\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"Comprehensive benchmarking and leaderboard of AI coding agents. Codex CLI on GPT-5.5 leads Terminal-Bench 2.1 at 83.4% accuracy. The report provides detailed performance analysis across different coding tasks and benchmarks, reflecting the maturation of AI coding agent capabilities.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"MorphLLM\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"Technical Platform\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://www.morphllm.com/best-ai-coding-agents-2026\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding agents\", \"benchmarking\", \"GPT-5.5\", \"performance evaluation\", \"Terminal-Bench\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Rewriting Bun in Rust: A Case Study in Agentic Engineering\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Jarred Sumner's detailed blog post about rewriting Bun from Zig to Rust using AI coding agents. The project used coordinated parallel agents and a conformance suite to automate the port. The rewrite took 11 days and consumed an estimated $165,000 in tokens, demonstrating how AI agents enable ambitious projects that were previously considered impractical.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (covering Jarred Sumner)\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"Bun\", \"Rust\", \"Zig\", \"agentic engineering\", \"AI coding agents\", \"Claude Code\", \"software rewrites\", \"conformance suites\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"Introducing GPT-Live: OpenAI's Voice Mode Upgrade\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"OpenAI upgraded the model used by ChatGPT voice mode to a frontier model with ability to delegate harder tasks to GPT-5.5. Simon Willison reports on the improved capabilities and practical experiences during the preview period, including the model's ability to maintain conversation flow while handling complex background tasks.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"GPT-Live\", \"OpenAI\", \"voice mode\", \"real-time AI\", \"ChatGPT\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   131→  },\n   132→  {\n   133→    \"title\": \"AI-Written Change Descriptions: A Moratorium\",\n   134→    \"description\": \"Kenton Varda declared a moratorium against AI-written change descriptions (PR and commit messages, issues/tickets) from his team. The insight: AI was writing worse-than-useful descriptions that outlined code details visible in the code itself while omitting the higher-level framing needed to understand what the code is doing.\",\n   135→    \"author\": \"Kenton Varda (via Simon Willison)\",\n   136→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   137→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net\",\n   138→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   139→    \"topics\": [\"AI code review\", \"PR descriptions\", \"commit messages\", \"AI limitations\", \"developer workflows\"],\n   140→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   141→  },\n   142→  {\n   143→    \"title\": \"How I Write Software with LLMs\",\n   144→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion exploring style differences between LLM and human-generated code, covering code density differences between AI and human developers and practical workflows.\",\n   145→    \"author\": \"Hacker News Community\",\n   146→    \"source\": \"Hacker News\",\n   147→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47394022\",\n   148→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   149→    \"topics\": [\"AI coding workflows\", \"LLM code generation\", \"code style\", \"developer experience\"],\n   150→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   151→  },\n   152→  {\n   153→    \"title\": \"sqlite-utils 4.0: Now with Database Schema Migrations\",\n   154→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison released sqlite-utils 4.0, the 124th release of the project and first major version bump since 2020. This version introduces database migrations, nested transactions via db.atomic(), and support for compound foreign keys. The release was heavily assisted by Claude Fable AI coding agent.\",\n   155→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   156→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   157→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net\",\n   158→    \"published_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   159→    \"topics\": [\"sqlite-utils\", \"database migrations\", \"SQLite\", \"AI-assisted programming\", \"Claude Fable\"],\n   160→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   161→  }\n   162→]\n   163→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
